 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DdOnSO0NLxI/",
 "title": "3 Things Harvey Specter Teaches About Corporate Power",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator explains three corporate power principles inspired by the fictional character Harvey Specter: leveraging information asymmetry, deal structuring, and signaling. These tactics give negotiators powerful advantages in business and finance.",
 "key_points": [
  "Information asymmetry: Knowing something the other person doesn't (a weakness, secret, or pressure point) gives you a powerful advantage because the person with better information almost always wins.",
  "Deal structuring (M&A): Instead of just negotiating price, restructure the entire transaction so terms favor your client before anyone starts talking about numbers.",
  "Signaling: Never show desperation or what losing looks like, because revealing how much you need something determines how much it costs you.",
  "Harvey Specter's core principles of leverage, framing, and signaling are instinctive to him but take most financial professionals years to learn."
 ],
 "content": "3 things HARVEY SPECTER teaches about CORPORATE POWER. I know Harvey Specter is a fictional lawyer, but I think he understands corporate power better than most of the people. Let me explain. First, Harvey never walks into a negotiation without leverage, because in every single scene, before he sits down, he most of the times already knows something that the other person doesn't. A weakness or a secret, basically a pressure point. And in corporate finance, that's called information asymmetry. And it's one of the most powerful advantages that you can have in any deal, because the person with the better information almost always wins. Harvey just makes it look effortless. Second, he never argues on someone else's terms, because in every negotiation, he reframes the conversation before it even starts. Basically changes what's being discussed. In fact, changes what even winning looks like. And in M&A, that's called deal structuring. Deal structuring means that the best bankers don't just negotiate the price; they restructure the entire transaction so that the terms favor their client before anyone even starts talking about numbers. And lastly, Harvey never shows what losing looks like. Not because he never loses, but because the moment you show desperation in a negotiation, the other side knows exactly how hard to push. And in finance, that's called signaling. What you reveal about how much you need something determines how much it costs you. So basically, leverage, framing, and signaling, three thing Harvey Specter does instinctively that takes most financial professionals years to learn. So all in all, he's fictional, but his principles aren't. You're welcome.",
